      "math_academy_id": "e-7826",
      "problem": "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are two events such that $P(B) = \\dfrac23$ and $P(A\\cap B) = \\dfrac13.$ What is $P(A|B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the definition of the conditional probability $P(A|B){:}$\n\n$$\nP(A|B) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{P(A|B)&=\\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n\t}\n$$",
